\

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Why This Matters
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
Lonelybot's MCTS does deep lookahead with hypothesis testing (sampling possible hidden card arrangements). No human can do this. We need a "strong human" baseline that plays using rules of thumb.\
\

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Approach A: Adapt an Existing Heuristic Solver
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 Daniel Collins' SolitaireSolver\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5  (Java, GitHub)\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Already runs heuristic play over 100K games per ruleset\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Reports ~7.6% on draw-3 with 3 passes (but draw-1 unlimited should be much higher)\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Repo: https://github.com/danielrcollins1/SolitaireSolver\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Could modify heuristics and run on draw-1 unlimited passes\
\
\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 Bill Turnbull's approach\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5  (not open source, but documented)\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Achieved 52.9% on draw-1 unlimited passes over 10M games\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Heuristic rules are partially documented online\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Could reverse-engineer the strategy\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Approach B: Build a Custom Heuristic Engine (Python)
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\
Build a Klondike engine from scratch with pluggable strategy modules:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \strokec2 ```\cf5 \strokec5 \
klondike_engine/\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  game.py          # Game state, card representation, move generation\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  deck.py          # Shuffling, dealing\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  tableau.py       # Tableau column logic\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  foundation.py    # Foundation pile logic\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  stock.py         # Stock/waste pile with draw-1, unlimited passes\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  solver.py        # Base solver interface\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  heuristic.py     # Rule-based heuristic player\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  simulator.py     # Run N games, collect stats\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  analytics.py     # Win rate, CI, EV calculations\
\uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  main.py          # CLI entry point\
\cf2 \strokec2 ```\cf5 \strokec5 \
\
\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 Core heuristic rules (priority order):\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 \
\cf2 \strokec2 1.\cf5 \strokec5  Always play an Ace to foundation\
\cf2 \strokec2 2.\cf5 \strokec5  Always play a 2 to foundation if the corresponding Ace is already there\
\cf2 \strokec2 3.\cf5 \strokec5  Play to foundation if both cards of the opposite color and one rank lower are already on foundations (safe foundation play)\
\cf2 \strokec2 4.\cf5 \strokec5  Prefer moves that reveal face-down cards\
\cf2 \strokec2 5.\cf5 \strokec5  Prefer moves that free up tableau columns\
\cf2 \strokec2 6.\cf5 \strokec5  Move Kings to empty columns (prefer Kings with the most face-down cards behind them)\
\cf2 \strokec2 7.\cf5 \strokec5  Build tableau sequences that uncover the most hidden cards\
\cf2 \strokec2 8.\cf5 \strokec5  Draw from stock as a last resort\
\cf2 \strokec2 9.\cf5 \strokec5  Track stock cycling to detect dead-end loops\
\
\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 Advanced heuristics:\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 \
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Don't send cards to foundation too eagerly (they may be needed for tableau building)\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Prefer longer tableau builds (more cards uncovered per move sequence)\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  When choosing between equivalent moves, prefer the one in the column with more face-down cards\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Detect "stuck" states (cycling stock with no new moves) and give up\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

