\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ##\cf4 \strokec4  Phase 3: Build a Human-Like Heuristic Player
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Why
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
Lonelybot's MCTS does deep lookahead that no human can replicate during a timed game. We need a "strong human" simulation to estimate what a disciplined, skilled player actually achieves.\
\

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Approach Option A: Adapt Existing Heuristic Solver
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  \cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 Daniel Collins' SolitaireSolver\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5  (Java, GPL): https://github.com/danielrcollins1/SolitaireSolver\
  \cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Runs heuristic play over 100K games per ruleset\
  \cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Already reports ~7.6% on draw-3 with 3 passes\
  \cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Adapt to draw-1 unlimited passes\
  \cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  His results for draw-1 unlimited: need to check/run\
\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  \cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5 Bill Turnbull's Solitaire Tester\cf2 \strokec2 **\cf5 \strokec5  (not open source but results published)\
  \cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Reports 52.9-53.4% on draw-1 unlimited passes with optimized heuristics\
  \cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  This is the benchmark to try to match\
\

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Approach Option B: Build Custom Python Heuristic Engine
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
Build a Python Klondike engine with pluggable strategy modules:\
\
\cf2 \strokec2 ```\cf5 \strokec5 \
klondike/\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  engine.py          # Core game state, legal move generation, card dealing\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  strategies/\
\uc0\u9474    \u9500 \u9472 \u9472  base.py        # Strategy interface\
\uc0\u9474    \u9500 \u9472 \u9472  greedy.py      # Always play to foundation when possible\
\uc0\u9474    \u9500 \u9472 \u9472  heuristic.py   # Weighted heuristic (see below)\
\uc0\u9474    \u9492 \u9472 \u9472  mcts.py        # Simple MCTS for comparison\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  simulator.py       # Run N games with a given strategy, collect stats\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  analyzer.py        # EV calculations, confidence intervals, visualizations\
\uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  main.py            # CLI entry point\
\cf2 \strokec2 ```\cf5 \strokec5 \
\

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Heuristic Priority Rules (Based on Published Klondike Strategy Research)
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\cf2 \strokec2 1.\cf5 \strokec5  Always play Aces and Twos to foundation immediately\
\cf2 \strokec2 2.\cf5 \strokec5  Prefer moves that reveal face-down cards\
\cf2 \strokec2 3.\cf5 \strokec5  Prefer moves that free up tableau columns\
\cf2 \strokec2 4.\cf5 \strokec5  Don't play cards to foundation if they're needed for tableau building (the "safe-to-play" rule: only send to foundation if both cards of the opposite color and one rank lower are already on foundations)\
\cf2 \strokec2 5.\cf5 \strokec5  Prefer moving cards from larger piles\
\cf2 \strokec2 6.\cf5 \strokec5  Among equal moves, prefer the one that increases the number of face-up cards\
\cf2 \strokec2 7.\cf5 \strokec5  Only draw from stock when no beneficial tableau moves exist\
\cf2 \strokec2 8.\cf5 \strokec5  When a column is empty, prefer moving Kings that expose face-down cards\
\cf2 \strokec2 9.\cf5 \strokec5  Between two Kings for an empty column, prefer the one with more face-down cards behind it\
\

\f0\b \cf2 \strokec2 ###\cf4 \strokec4  Sample Size
\f1\b0 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Run 100,000+ games per heuristic configuration\
\cf2 \strokec2 -\cf5 \strokec5  Python will be slower than Rust but still manageable (estimate ~1-5 seconds/game depending on implementation)\
